1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,699
Thursday afternoon.

2
00:00:01,749 --> 00:00:02,549
Rob.

3
00:00:05,789 --> 00:00:07,982
The problem: warnings can be ignored,

4
00:00:08,032 --> 00:00:11,255
and some standards must never
be broken in production.

5
00:00:11,305 --> 00:00:16,226
This part shows the Kubernetes API server
itself refusing a change that breaks the

6
00:00:16,276 --> 00:00:18,348
rules, and then the week in review.

7
00:00:19,521 --> 00:00:20,321
Thursday.

8
00:00:20,079 --> 00:00:24,925
Warnings can be ignored, and some standards
must never be broken in production.

9
00:00:24,975 --> 00:00:28,582
This is the one place where
governance can say no outright,

10
00:00:28,632 --> 00:00:31,619
and it is a capability
only the Tyk Operator has.

11
00:00:32,786 --> 00:00:36,876
For the payments category, Rob
decides that warnings aren't enough.

12
00:00:36,926 --> 00:00:40,646
He changes one word in the
ruleset: the action becomes block.

13
00:00:41,806 --> 00:00:42,637
He applies it.

14
00:00:42,687 --> 00:00:44,777
Then someone tries a bad rollback:

15
00:00:44,827 --> 00:00:48,617
pushing Monday's insecure version
of the payments spec again.

16
00:00:49,755 --> 00:00:52,515
The Kubernetes API server
refused the change.

17
00:00:52,565 --> 00:00:57,073
The Tyk Operator's admission webhook
checked it, found twenty-two errors,

18
00:00:57,123 --> 00:01:00,007
and denied the request,
listing the first five.

19
00:01:00,057 --> 00:01:02,380
The insecure version was never stored,

20
00:01:02,430 --> 00:01:05,502
so it never reached the
Dashboard or the gateways.

21
00:01:06,656 --> 00:01:10,493
The payments API is still running
Tuesday's compliant version.

22
00:01:14,955 --> 00:01:17,623
In the Dashboard the
ruleset still shows warn.

23
00:01:17,673 --> 00:01:20,223
Blocking is something
only the cluster does,

24
00:01:20,273 --> 00:01:23,709
so the Dashboard and the pipeline
behave exactly as before.

25
00:01:25,085 --> 00:01:27,279
Rob sets it back to warn for now.

26
00:01:28,448 --> 00:01:29,534
Friday morning.

27
00:01:34,067 --> 00:01:34,867
Friday.

28
00:01:34,532 --> 00:01:39,331
The Kestrel Payments API is compliant
and the partner launch is on track.

29
00:01:39,381 --> 00:01:41,324
Rob never reviewed it by hand.

30
00:01:42,471 --> 00:01:46,911
Everything lives in the cluster
as code: the rulesets, the APIs,

31
00:01:46,961 --> 00:01:49,437
and a governance status on each one.

32
00:01:54,219 --> 00:01:58,642
And the Dashboard turns the estate's
compliance into a number anyone can read.

33
00:02:00,044 --> 00:02:01,257
The week in review.

34
00:02:01,307 --> 00:02:04,383
Monday, Rob defined the
standard once, as code.

35
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:06,710
Tuesday, Ines found out on her own,

36
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:10,168
fixed her API with the
instructions in front of her,

37
00:02:10,218 --> 00:02:13,891
and handed one bad rule back
to Rob, who fixed the rule.

38
00:02:13,941 --> 00:02:17,482
Wednesday, the same check
moved into the pull request.

39
00:02:17,532 --> 00:02:21,738
Thursday, the rules that matter
most became impossible to break.

40
00:02:21,788 --> 00:02:27,257
And on Friday, compliance is a number on a
screen instead of a queue on Rob's desk.
